    "lawSection": "Real Property",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6481\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to clarify qualifications for certain supplemental costs associ-\nated with an emergency rental assistance program\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo make clarifying amendments to chapter 53 of the laws of 2021, enact-\ning the Aid to Localities Budget, in relation to qualifications for\ncertain supplemental costs associated with an emergency rental assist-\nance program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends chapter 53 of the laws of 2021 enacting the aid to\nlocalities budget and providing for supplemental costs associated with\nan emergency rental assistance program to clarify qualifications for\nsuch supplemental costs. Under the provisions of this section, rental\nassistance would be available:\n  (a) for tenant households who would qualify for the COVID-19 Emergency\nRental Assistance Program (CERAP) but for the fact that their income\nexceeds 80% of the area median income;\n  (b) for landlords with rental arrears accrued by a tenant who would\nhave qualified for assistance under CERAP or under subdivision (a) of\nthis section if the tenant had applied for assistance while in occupan-\ncy, if the landlord has used best efforts to contact and assist the\ntenant in applying, without success, including instances in which the\ntenant has vacated while owing rent; and\n  (c) to supplement funding for CERAP.\n\nSection two provides that the commissioner of the office of temporary\nand disability assistance (OTDA) shall establish procedures to verify\nthat information submitted by landlords to establish eligibility is\ncomplete and accurate and shall establish procedures to ensure flexibil-\nity when determining acceptable documentation including self-attestation\nand attestation by other knowledgeable parties.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe recently enacted budget provided $2.3 billion, using federal funds,\nto create the COVID-19 Emergency Rental Assistance Program (CERAP),\nwhich will cover up to a year of rent arrears and three additional\nmonths of prospective rent payments for tenant household who meet\ncertain requirements, including that their household income for 2020 or\nat the time they apply for the program does not exceed 80% of the area\nmedian income.\n\nThe budget also included an additional $100 million in State funds to\nsupplement CERAP. This bill clarifies and expands eligibility for the\nsupplemental funds.\n\nFirst it ensures that tenant applicants will be eligible if they meet\nall the eligibility requirements of CERAP except that their income\nexceeds 80% of the area median. This bill makes clear that certain\npriorities included in CERAP also apply to the supplemental funds, but\nthat as with CERAP, a household can qualify for assistance without being\nincluded among the priority households. This provision is especially\nimportant in areas of the state where many tenants have income exceeding\nthe 80% area median income threshold.\n\nSecond, this bill addresses situations where a landlord has made a good\nfaith effort to contact a tenant and assist the tenant in applying for\nrental assistance and either the tenant has declined to apply or the\nlandlord has been unable to contact the tenant, including instances in\nwhich the tenant has already vacated the residence. The CERAP program is\ndesigned to encourage both landlords and tenants to make their best\nefforts to ensure complete applications are submitted for rental assist-\nance -- and even tenants who have vacated their residence may be eligi-\nble to cover rent arrears they still owe -- but the State supplemental\nfunds and the provisions of this bill are important because federal law\nprecludes spending CERAP funds on rent accrued by tenants who do not\napply.  Covering such arrears may be critical for landlords who have\nacted in good faith throughout the pandemic to cover the costs of mort-\ngages, maintenance, taxes, utilities, and other expenses, especially\nsmall landlords.\n\nThe bill also directs the commissioner of OTDA to establish procedures\nto ensure information submitted by landlords to prove eligibility is\naccurate and supported by documentation. The provisions of this bill\nregarding documentation requirements for landlord applicants mirror the\nprovisions applicable to tenant applicants in CERAP.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
